# The sensors HAL creates the lid switch as a uinput device, and was denied.
#
#   E sensors-hal: mot_flip:122, mot_flip: failed to open uinput: Permission denied
#   avc: denied { write } for comm="android.hardwar" name="uinput" dev="tmpfs"
#        scontext=u:r:hal_sensors_default:s0 tcontext=u:object_r:uhid_device:s0
#        tclass=chr_file permissive=0
#
# That is the BINDING constraint on this device's fold logic, not the hinge
# sensor. MotFlip_LID (SW_LID) is a uinput node the HAL creates; denied, it never
# appears, and EventHub's whole inventory is gpio-keys, pmic_pwrkey, pmic_resin,
# double-tap, s-double-tap, gdx_cli_0, goodix_ts -- no lid.
#
# EVERY one of the seven states in fold/device_state_configuration.xml carries a
# <lid-switch> condition, and DeviceStateProviderImpl's LidSwitchBooleanSupplier
# throws IllegalStateException("Have not received lid switch value") while
# mIsLidOpen is null, so the state is skipped. States 1-5 are additionally hard
# false because com.motorola.sensor.hinge_posture is missing. Result:
#   DeviceStateProviderImpl: No declared device states match any of the required conditions
#   DeviceStateManagerService: Cannot notify device state info change before the
#                              initial state has been committed
# and the device state stays INVALID (= STATE_DEFAULT = -1), so
# display_layout_configuration.xml is parsed but NEVER APPLIED.
#
# With this rule, and even with the hinge sensor still broken, the provider falls
# through states 1-5 and lands on state 6 OPENED_HALL (lid-only, evaluated last):
# inner primary, outer disabled -- the correct open-device behaviour.
allow hal_sensors_default uhid_device:chr_file rw_file_perms;

# vendor.camera.led.torchmode, moved off vendor_default_prop by property.te so
# that the camera provider can set it at all. THIS GRANT IS THE OTHER HALF OF
# THAT CHANGE, not a new capability: sensors.qsh.so is one of only two binaries
# in the firmware that reference the string, and before the relabel it read the
# property for free through the blanket domain.te:508 vendor_default_prop grant.
# Without this the sensors HAL silently loses a read it has today.
#
# `set` as well as `get` because that is what stock gives it
# (vendor_sepolicy.cil:14108-14109), not because a write was observed here.
set_prop(hal_sensors_default, vendor_camera_ledtorchmode_prop)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE Motorola sensors_class ENUMERATION -- 140 denials per boot (130 file
# reads + 10 dir reads), all in one burst at HAL start:
#
# ⚠️ COUNT CORRECTED 2026-09-01, measured on the shipping build rather than
# estimated: exactly 140 distinct audit serials, 130 tclass=file + 10 tclass=dir,
# the whole burst spanning 4 ms at HAL start. That matches the arithmetic below
# exactly -- 13 attributes x 10 devices + the 10 parent directories = 140. The
# earlier "142 (132 file + 10 dir)" never agreed with its own breakdown.
#
#   avc: denied { read } for name="max_range"/"resolution"/"sensor_power"/
#        "poll_delay"/"min_delay"/"max_delay"/"flags"/"fifo_max_event_count"/
#        "fifo_reserved_event_count"/"name"/"vendor"/"version"/"type"
#        dev="sysfs" scontext=u:r:hal_sensors_default:s0
#        tcontext=u:object_r:sysfs:s0 tclass=file
#   avc: denied { read } for name="input4".."input13" dir (same contexts)
#
# 13 attributes x 10 devices + the 10 parent directories. Located on the live
# device: /sys/class/sensors is already u:object_r:vendor_sysfs_sensors:s0, but
# its entries are symlinks into the input tree, and the real directories are
#
#   /sys/devices/virtual/input/input{4..13}/<sensor name>/
#
# with names "Moto CapSense Ch0".."Ch6", "Moto_Top_Approach_Det", "dt-gesture"
# and "s-dt-gesture" -- ten of them, matching the ten denied parents exactly.
#
# WHY A GRANT AND NOT genfscon. Two independent blockers:
#   1. genfs_contexts entries are whitespace-separated, and seven of the ten
#      directory names contain SPACES. There is no way to spell
#      ".../input4/Moto CapSense Ch1" in that file at all.
#   2. The inputN index is assigned at driver probe, so a per-device prefix is
#      no more stable than the i2c bus numbering documented in genfs_contexts.
# Stock solves it with a subtree label -- (genfscon sysfs "/devices/virtual/input"
# (u object_r sysfs_input)) at vendor_sepolicy.cil:444, plus
# `allow hal_sensors sysfs_input {dir file lnk_file}` -- but sysfs_input is a
# Motorola type that exists in neither AOSP nor QCOM's sepolicy_vndr here, and
# adopting it would relabel EVERY virtual input device, including the uinput
# nodes the fold logic depends on and the dt-gesture nodes vendor_init writes.
# That is a change whose blast radius cannot be checked without a flash; a
# read-only grant on the generic type has none.
#
# COSMETIC. Verified live: `dumpsys sensorservice` lists 68 hardware sensors, all
# running, including Hinge Angle, Hinge Posture, Flip Position, Flip Hall Effect
# and both Flip Approach/Presence. The denied nodes are the SAR capsense
# channels' sysfs description, not the sensor data path.
# ⚠️ DISABLED 2026-09-01 -- CONFIRMED BY EXPERIMENT. Keep them disabled.
#
# After these three grants landed, double-tap-to-wake stopped waking the phone on
# BOTH panels. Measured at that point: the touch driver still DETECTS the gesture
# (ts_mmi_gesture_handler logs "double tap; x=,y=" and the input device's ABS_X/
# ABS_Y update to exactly those coordinates), but `getevent -c 1` on
# /dev/input/event12 blocks forever -- NOT ONE event packet reaches an evdev
# client, so no KEYCODE_WAKEUP is ever generated.
#
# Hypothesis: these grants let the sensor stack finish enumerating and CLAIM the
# gesture evdev nodes. `dumpsys sensorservice` now lists them as sensors --
#   0x01000007 dt-gesture    com.motorola.sensor.double_tap
#   0x0100000a s-dt-gesture  com.motorola.sensor.double_tap
# -- and motorola.hardware.sensorext-service is running. A grabbed device
# delivers to nobody else, which matches the measurement exactly.
#
# This is the LOAD-BEARING DENIAL pattern: the denial was doing useful work, and
# removing it unblocked a path we did not want. Same shape as the thermal-engine
# proc_pid_max case earlier today.
#
# RESULT: with these three lines commented out, the dt-gesture/s-dt-gesture
# sensors DISAPPEAR from `dumpsys sensorservice` and double-tap-to-wake WORKS
# again on both panels (operator-confirmed). Causation is proven, both directions.
#
# DECISION: leave them disabled. The trade is explicit --
#   cost: 140 denials/boot return -- measured 2026-09-01, not estimated; the
#         earlier "~118" here was a guess. They are COSMETIC (see the note
#         above: the denied nodes are the SAR capsense channels' sysfs
#         DESCRIPTION, not the sensor data path).
#         ⚠️ Do not quote the sensor counts that used to sit on this line.
#         "68" and "92" have both been written down in this tree and neither was
#         reproducible. Measured on the shipping grants-off build,
#         `dumpsys sensorservice` lists 79 sensors (101 on the stock capture in
#         workspace/contract/open/sensorservice.txt). That gap is a separate
#         question and is NOT evidence about this trade either way.
#   gain: a real, user-facing feature works.
# Log noise never outranks a working feature.
#
# ⚠️ DO NOT "fix" these denials again without re-testing double-tap-to-wake.
# That is the whole point of this comment.
#
# A narrower grant is not available: the capsense nodes cannot be spelled in
# genfs_contexts (seven of ten directory names contain SPACES, and inputN is
# assigned at probe), which is why the grant had to be on the generic sysfs type
# and therefore also covered the gesture nodes.
#
# If someone later wants BOTH, the durable route is to stop fighting for the evdev
# node and consume the com.motorola.sensor.double_tap SENSOR instead -- which is
# how stock does it, and is immune to who owns the input device.
#
# allow hal_sensors_default sysfs:dir r_dir_perms;
# allow hal_sensors_default sysfs:file r_file_perms;
# allow hal_sensors_default sysfs:lnk_file r_file_perms;

# NOT FIXABLE: hal_sensors_default reads sys.boot_completed
#   avc: denied { read } for name="u:object_r:boot_status_prop:s0"
#        tcontext=u:object_r:boot_status_prop:s0
# boot_status_prop is system_restricted_prop (public/property.te:60). A
# `restricted` type only neverallows coredomain WRITES, so get_prop() from a
# vendor domain would in principle build -- but AOSP deliberately keeps this one
# core-only and the HAL works without it (it is a "wait for boot" convenience).
# Left alone rather than widened; 2 denials per boot.
